[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[4A\r\u001b[J\r\nThe median test now passes, and the full suite runs clean (4 tests, OK).\r\n\r\n\u001b[1mThe\u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mbug:\u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m\u001b[36mmedian\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0min \u001b[36mstats.py\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0mtook \u001b[36mordered[len\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m//\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m2]\u001b[0m. That is correct for odd-length lists, but for\r\neven-length lists it returned only the upper of the two middle values. For \u001b[36m[4,\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m1,\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m3,\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m2]\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0mit returned\r\n\u001b[36m3\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0minstead of \u001b[36m2.5\u001b[0m.\r\n\r\n\u001b[1mThe\u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mfix:\u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0mFor an even count, \u001b[36mmedian\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0mnow returns the mean of the two middle values. Odd counts still\r\nreturn the middle element. The change is confined to \u001b[36mmedian\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0min \u001b[36mstats.py\u001b[0m; I did not modify the tests.\r\n\r\n\u001b[1mVerification:\u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m\u001b[36mpython3\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m-m\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36munittest\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m-q\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0mpreviously failed with \u001b[36m3\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m!=\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m2.5\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0mand n"]
