[0.0, "o", "\u001b[?2026h\u001b[4A\r\u001b[J\r\nBoth parts are done, and I checked the final state myself: 7 tests pass with \u001b[36mpython3\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m-m\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36munittest\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0m\u001b[36m-q\u001b[0m.\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;36mmode()\u001b[0m\u001b[1;36m \u001b[0m\u001b[1m(added\u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mby\u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mmode-agent)\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m- \u001b[0m\u001b[36mstats.py\u001b[0m: \u001b[36mmode(values)\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0mreturns the most frequent value. On a tie it returns the smallest tied\r\n  value. Empty input raises \u001b[36mValueError\u001b[0m.\r\n\u001b[2m- \u001b[0m\u001b[36mtest_stats.py\u001b[0m: three new tests cover a clear winner, a tie, and the empty-input error.\r\n\u001b[2m- \u001b[0mLimit: it uses a dict, so values must be hashable. Numbers and strings work; lists do not.\r\n\r\n\u001b[1;36mREADME.md\u001b[0m\u001b[1;36m \u001b[0m\u001b[1m(written\u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mby\u001b[0m\u001b[1m \u001b[0m\u001b[1mreadme-agent)\u001b[0m\r\n\r\n\u001b[2m- \u001b[0mCovers the module's purpose, each function's behaviour and error cases, a usage example, and how\r\n  to run the tests.\r\n\u001b[2m- \u001b[0mThe examples were run against the code, and the README matches the current \u001b[36mmode\u001b[0m\u001b[36m \u001b[0mtie-breaking a"]
